## Engineering principles (non-negotiable)

Whatever the mechanisms in `docs/*` say, the following hold. Where the scope is limited, state that within the entry.

<!-- Tag each entry: "enforced by <gate>" or "review-only".
     That review-only column is the standing pool of guardrail candidates — next time one is
     violated, take it out of the pool and pin it. -->

<!-- Draw on these according to the project's actual situation. Prefer few and accurate over
     many and hollow.
     The shape of each entry: conclusion (bold) + concrete form + why + link to the detail +
     mechanical enforcement note. -->

- **TDD/BDD first — write the failing test before changing observable behaviour.** Describe the behaviour with `Given/When/Then` or BDD-style `describe`/`it`, covering the happy path plus at least one edge or failure case **the contract genuinely owns** (the criterion is the gate in [`docs/testing.md`](docs/testing.md#covering-the-behaviour-space-deliberately) — if the contract really has no edge, write only the happy path rather than manufacturing one), **and then** write the implementation. The two exceptions (a genuinely behaviour-preserving refactor, and automation genuinely not being feasible) are in [`docs/testing.md`](docs/testing.md#exceptions-to-tdd) — **not** a blanket rule by file or task category.

- **Confirm the bug really exists before fixing it.** Reproduce it first, **with a failure reason matching the report**; fix the reproduction into a failing test, then change the implementation. When you cannot reproduce it, **say so plainly and stop** — do not fix a hallucinated bug, and do not "fix it roughly like this". The order is: confirm it exists → write the test / record the evidence → fix.

- **Fix the root cause, do not patch.** Do not add a guard at the consumer to paper over a bad value from the producer; do not normalise the same field repeatedly at several call sites — normalise once at the boundary. Refactoring **the unit you are currently changing** is in scope, which is a different thing from the drive-by refactoring banned below.

- **Scope discipline — touch only what this task needs.** One change = the producer + its tests + at most one drive-by drift fix (an obvious one-line error). **Banned**: smuggled-in refactors / rename sweeps / formatting / import reordering / unrelated test changes — they drown the real change and break `git bisect`. When you see unrelated mess, **report it and ask; do not fix it in passing**. Three lines of similar code beat a premature abstraction.

- **Reuse first — grep before writing.** Two parallel implementations drift within weeks. A decidable heuristic: a new file importing both a UI primitive **and** a data store is probably rebuilding <existing component>; copying more than 10 lines → extract it; the same fix appearing in two similar blocks → **the second one is a bug**, so delete it and call the first. <List this repository's existing shared entry points: components, hooks, utilities, cross-cutting concerns>

- **SOLID / high cohesion, low coupling — landing on this repository's existing extension points.** <Write each as a concrete shape in this repository, e.g.:>
  - <Adding an X → implement `<interface name>` and `Register()` it in `init()`; **do not `switch` on a type string in shared code** — that is exactly the coupling the registry exists to remove.>
  - <Depend on interfaces, obtained through a getter; a service must not import another service's repository.>
  - <Dependency direction is one-way: `A → B → C`; `<cross-cutting layer>` is a leaf and must not import backwards.>
  *<The mechanically checkable part has been enforced by `<check name>` since `<date>` (running inside `<command>`); the exemption list **only shrinks**.>*

- **No defensive code — a pointless fallback is a liability.** Defences written for the impossible, swallowed errors and shims left for retired data all turn into noise nobody dares delete, while the real bug hides behind the guard.
  - **Validate at boundaries only** — <list this repository's boundaries: external input / IPC entry points / plugin host calls>. Internal calls trust each other; do not write `if x == nil`.
  - **Do not swallow errors** — `if err != nil { return nil }` / `catch { return defaultState }` masks failure and propagates dirty state.
  - **Do not double-fallback a user-configurable field** — `value || "default"` overrides a deliberate empty value from the user and masks bugs in the helper.
  - **Do not leave runtime shims for retired data** — a migration runs once, so delete the field from the model.

- **Direct replacement beats an adapter sandwich.** When swapping a backend or a library, **replace it in place**; do not build `interface Foo + LegacyImpl + NewImpl` unless the two genuinely have to coexist at runtime. A sandwich forces every call site to work out "which layer am I on now".

- **Leave no dead code and no deletion markers.** Git remembers. Delete it outright; do not leave `_deprecated` placeholders.

- **Before adding a dependency, ask whether what exists can do it.** A new dependency is a permanent maintenance and security burden. When one genuinely is needed: the lockfile must be committed; one package manager only (<this repository's enforcement mechanism>); and explain in the PR why the existing approach was insufficient.

- **Credentials and sensitive data stay out of the repository, the logs and the reports.** Secrets go in `.env` (already gitignored) or <secret management approach>. Redact logs and verification reports before pasting them anywhere — they have far more readers than you imagine. <This project's specific data red lines: e.g. personal / order / financial data.>

- **Critical paths must be observable.** <External calls / state changes / permission decisions / failure degradations> emit structured logs carrying a correlation id — so that when something goes wrong the logs can reconstruct "what actually happened" instead of you guessing. How to choose a level, where to instrument and what must never be logged are in [`docs/observability.md`](docs/observability.md).

